## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests practical testing skills around observability and reliability in real services, focusing on privacy and resilience rather than pure throughput.

## Key Concepts

- Logging contracts and payload schemas
- PIi redaction and data minimization
- Resilience: timeouts, retries, fallbacks
- Test pyramids: unit/integration/contract

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test outline for log formatter
describe('log formatter', () => {
  it('redacts PII', () => {
    const input = { email: 'user@example.com', card: '4111111111111111' };
    const out = redactPII(input);
    expect(out.email).toBe('[REDACTED]');
    expect(out.card).toBe('[REDACTED]');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate logger 429 responses in tests?
- How would you evolve the JSON schema over time without breaking consumers?

## Why This Is Asked
High-scale streaming systems require robust end-to-end validation that goes beyond unit testing. Organizations need assurance that their pipelines handle schema evolution, late data, and data drift while maintaining consistency between staging and production environments.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end data validation across landing areas and warehouse
- Idempotent MERGE/upsert semantics ensuring data consistency
- Late data and backfill handling with drift detection mechanisms
- Traffic simulation and CI-backed regression testing
- Schema evolution compatibility validation
- Data reconciliation and consistency checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test: hash payload for idempotency validation
const crypto = require('crypto');
function hashPayload(payload) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256')
    .update(JSON.stringify(payload))
    .digest('hex');
}

// Validate idempotent upserts by comparing hash signatures
```

